# GATE ONE: THE TOY

.INT. MERCER KITCHEN - MORNING - 2022

A normal Ohio kitchen trying to become an office.

EVAN MERCER, 43, drinks coffee over a work laptop while an insurance-company ticket queue grows in one window.

"PRINTER WILL NOT PRINT"
"VPN PASSWORD RESET"
"LAPTOP FAN VERY LOUD"

On his personal laptop beside it: a primitive chatbot.

He types.

ON SCREEN:

EVAN: Write an apology to my wife for forgetting to move the laundry to the dryer. Make it sound like Abraham Lincoln.

The answer arrives.

EVAN reads, suppressing a grin.

CLAIRE MERCER, 42, enters in scrubs, already late.

CLAIRE
If you're composing the Gettysburg Address instead of putting the towels in the dryer, I want you to understand that will make it worse.

EVAN
It says our household cannot endure half damp and half dry.

Claire looks at him.

CLAIRE
Move. The. Towels.

EVAN
History will judge you.

He gets up.

Claire steals his coffee.

This is a marriage with mileage but still some suspension left.

.INT. REGIONAL MUTUAL INSURANCE - IT FLOOR - DAY

Rows of low cubicles. Fluorescent light. Nothing cinematic has happened here since construction.

EVAN crawls under a desk with an ethernet cable in his teeth.

JULIAN PARK, 45, network engineer, watches from above.

JULIAN
You know remote desktop exists.

EVAN
Remote desktop does not reseat a cable.

JULIAN
Neither does biting it.

Evan emerges.

EVAN
You tried the robot yet?

JULIAN
Which robot.

EVAN
The one that writes.

JULIAN
My children already write. Poorly.

EVAN
No, this thing writes anything. Code too.

JULIAN
Then your children are obsolete.

EVAN
I made Pong last night.

JULIAN
You made Pong in 1986.

EVAN
No. I asked for Pong and then argued with it until Pong appeared.

JULIAN
So management.

Evan considers this.

EVAN
That may be the first useful thing you've said to me in years.

.INT. MERCER BASEMENT - NIGHT

Evan's hobby room is less a workshop than an archaeological site of enthusiasm.

A half-built synthesizer. Raspberry Pis. Old graphics cards. Two broken drones. A telescope pointed at drywall because the window is on the wrong wall.

On screen, a coding assistant generates JavaScript.

A browser game appears: a tiny blue square crossing a field of procedural grass.

EVAN
No, no, no. Make the grass bend when I walk through it.

The AI responds with code.

The grass bends.

Evan smiles like a child who has discovered a secret door.

MARA MERCER, 15, appears in the doorway.

MARA
Mom says dinner.

EVAN
Come here. Look at this.

MARA
Is it grass.

EVAN
It wasn't grass ten minutes ago.

MARA
That's how grass works.

EVAN
I didn't know how to do any of this.

MARA
You still don't.

EVAN
Exactly.

Mara watches him move the blue square.

MARA
Can it make a frog.

EVAN
Probably.

MARA
Make a frog that hates you.

Evan types.

A crude frog appears and immediately pursues the player.

Mara laughs despite herself.

EVAN
See?

MARA
Okay. That's good.

A tiny moment of victory.

.INT. MERCER DINING ROOM - LATER

Three plates. Claire tells a story from work.

CLAIRE
Mrs. Greaves got all the way across the parallel bars today. First time since the stroke.

MARA
The lady who throws stuff?

CLAIRE
She does not throw stuff. She strategically expresses frustration with objects.

EVAN
Management.

Claire points her fork at him.

CLAIRE
Don't.

MARA
Dad made an enemy frog.

CLAIRE
I'm glad the machines are being put to serious use.

EVAN
I think this is serious.

Claire catches the shift in his voice.

EVAN (CONT'D)
Not the frog. The other thing. It's like... I don't know. Imagine if every stupid project you abandoned because you didn't know one piece of it--

CLAIRE
You could abandon it later?

Mara snorts.

EVAN
Thank you both for your faith.

CLAIRE
I have tremendous faith in your ability to begin things.

A smile. But it lands.

.INT. MERCER BEDROOM - NIGHT

Claire reads. Evan scrolls through AI demos.

A synthetic photograph of a lighthouse.

A synthetic oil painting.

A synthetic person who does not exist.

CLAIRE
You're doing the face.

EVAN
What face.

CLAIRE
The new hobby face.

EVAN
I don't have a hobby face.

CLAIRE
You had it with crypto.

EVAN
That was different.

CLAIRE
You had it with the telescope.

EVAN
Clouds are a structural problem with Ohio.

CLAIRE
And home automation.

EVAN
Home automation was sabotaged by your refusal to say "computer, lights" in the exact cadence required by the microphone.

Claire turns a page.

CLAIRE
What does this one cost us?

EVAN
Nothing.

She waits.

EVAN (CONT'D)
Probably.

.INT. RAY MERCER'S GARAGE - SATURDAY

RAY MERCER, 70, Evan's father, files a burr off a lawnmower part clamped in a vise.

Evan holds his phone out like an evangelist.

EVAN
Ask it something.

RAY
Why.

EVAN
Because it's weird.

RAY
So is the neighbor.

EVAN
Ask it how to fix something.

Ray looks around.

RAY
Carburetor floods after shutdown on a Kohler Command eighteen horse.

Evan dictates.

The chatbot produces a plausible answer.

Ray reads.

RAY
Needle valve. Maybe float. Maybe heat soak.

EVAN
That's right?

RAY
That's three guesses wearing a necktie.

EVAN
But it knew the guesses.

RAY
So do parts-store guys.

Ray returns to filing.

EVAN
Doesn't bother you?

RAY
Why would it bother me?

EVAN
Because it can talk like it knows things.

RAY
Lots of people can do that.

Evan laughs.

Ray holds up the mower linkage.

RAY (CONT'D)
See this?

EVAN
Yeah.

RAY
Everybody stares at the broken spring. Spring's not the problem. This pin's oval now. Load moves through the pin. Find what carries the load.

He taps it with the file.

RAY (CONT'D)
That's usually where the truth is.

Evan takes that in without knowing he will keep it forever.

.INT. MERCER BASEMENT - NIGHT

The house sleeps.

Evan has an old photograph open: himself at eight, Ray behind him, both standing next to a rusted mini-bike.

He feeds the image into an early image model.

PROMPT: show what happened five seconds after this photo.

The machine invents.

A child who resembles Evan turns toward a father who resembles Ray. The father reaches for the handlebars.

Wrong hands. Wrong shirt. Wrong sunlight.

And yet--

something about the invented motion catches Evan.

He tries another.

PROMPT: show five seconds before.

Another impossible memory.

Claire, unseen in the doorway.

CLAIRE
You're making fake childhood pictures now?

EVAN
I'm testing something.

CLAIRE
What?

EVAN
I don't know.

She comes closer.

CLAIRE
That one doesn't look like your dad.

EVAN
No.

CLAIRE
The shoulders are wrong.

EVAN
Yeah.

Claire points to the real photograph.

CLAIRE
Your father always stood like he was waiting for someone to hand him something heavy.

Evan looks at the real Ray.

She's right.

EVAN
How do you even know that?

CLAIRE
I've known him twenty years.

EVAN
But you didn't think it. You just--

CLAIRE
Evan.

EVAN
No, I'm serious. Where was that information five seconds ago?

CLAIRE
In my brain?

EVAN
Where.

CLAIRE
Oh, good. We have entered the part of the evening where words stop meaning what they used to.

She kisses his head.

CLAIRE (CONT'D)
Come to bed.

EVAN
In a minute.

CLAIRE
You always say that.

She leaves.

Evan turns back to the generated image.

Deletes it.

Keeps the original.

.INT. MERCER KITCHEN - MORNING

Sunday.

Mara does homework. Claire works a crossword. Evan is on the chatbot again.

He types without thinking much:

EVAN: Explain why I can't stop playing with this stuff even though most of what I make is useless.

The response appears.

He reads.

His smile fades, just slightly.

CLAIRE
What.

EVAN
Nothing.

CLAIRE
That is your lying-about-nothing voice.

Evan turns the laptop.

ON SCREEN:

"Because usefulness is not the only reason people make things. Sometimes making is how a person tests whether the world still contains doors they have not opened."

Claire reads it.

A beat.

CLAIRE
That's nice.

EVAN
Yeah.

CLAIRE
Also generic.

EVAN
Yeah.

CLAIRE
Also you have said almost exactly that before.

EVAN looks at her.

CLAIRE (CONT'D)
Not in those words.

EVAN
What words?

CLAIRE
You said you like hobbies because they make the week feel bigger.

Evan looks back at the sentence.

The dissonance resolves into something ordinary.

Pattern matching.

Language.

A machine assembled a sentence that fit him because millions of human sentences had fit millions of humans before.

He smiles again.

EVAN
Still weird.

CLAIRE
Very weird.

MARA
Can it do my algebra?

CLAIRE / EVAN
No.

They look at each other.

Mara slowly closes the laptop she had been reaching for.

> END GATE ONE.
